(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["multimodal",{"c:vision-language-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>vision-language model (VLM)\u003c/b> is an LLM that can also read images. A \u003cb>vision encoder\u003c/b> cuts the image into small patches and turns each into a vector; a \u003cb>projector\u003c/b> maps those vectors into the LLM's embedding space; the LLM then reads them as \u003cb>image tokens\u003c/b> alongside your text. VLMs are strong at reading text, charts, screenshots and documents, and weak at exact counting, precise positions and tiny details, so you test them on your own images before you trust them.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How an image becomes tokens\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Image\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Patches\u003c/b>\u003csmall>e.g. 14 or 28 px squares\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Vision encoder\u003c/b>\u003csmall>ViT\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Projector\u003c/b>\u003csmall>small MLP\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>LLM\u003c/b>\u003csmall>image tokens + text tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Text answer\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The vision encoder is usually a \u003cb>Vision Transformer (ViT)\u003c/b>: the image is split into a grid of patches, each patch is flattened into a vector and the transformer lets patches attend to each other. A classic example is LLaVA-1.5 (2023), which used a CLIP ViT encoder at 336 × 336 pixels with 14-pixel patches: 24 × 24 = \u003cb>576 image tokens\u003c/b> per image, passed through a two-layer MLP into a Vicuna LLM.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Training typically has two stages: first align the projector on image-caption pairs, then \u003cb>visual instruction tuning\u003c/b> on question-answer conversations about images (LLaVA generated much of its data with a text-only GPT-4). Frontier models now pretrain on images and text together from the start, and handle \u003cb>dynamic resolution\u003c/b>: instead of squashing every image to one size, they tile it or process it at native aspect ratio, so a tall receipt and a wide dashboard both stay legible. That is why image token counts vary with size, which the next lesson turns into money.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Calling a VLM\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Most providers and local servers (Ollama, vLLM) accept images in the OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions format as a content part, either a URL or a base64 data URL:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import base64\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # or OpenAI(base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\") for a local VLM\n\ndef ask_image(path: str, question: str) -&gt; str:\n    b64 = base64.b64encode(open(path, \"rb\").read()).decode()\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"&lt;vision-model&gt;\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n            {\"type\": \"image_url\", \"image_url\": {\"url\": f\"data:image/png;base64,{b64}\"}},\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": question},\n        ]}],\n        temperature=0,\n    )\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\nprint(ask_image(\"q3_revenue_chart.png\",\n                \"Read the y-axis label and unit first. Then give the value of the 'EU' bar for Q3.\"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Putting the image before the question and asking the model to \u003cb>read first, then answer\u003c/b> (transcribe the axis, legend or field labels before computing anything) is a cheap prompt change that often improves accuracy; measure it on your own images, as the lab in this module does.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What they are good and bad at\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Good at\u003c/th>\u003cth>Weak at\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mitigation\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reading printed text, labels, UI screenshots\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Very small text after the image is downscaled\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Send at higher resolution or crop the region\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Describing scenes, classifying images\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact counts of many small objects\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ask for a list of items, or use a detection model\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Charts: trends, comparisons, approximate values\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Precise values between gridlines\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ask for the source data; tolerate ranges\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tables and forms in clean documents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rotated, blurry or low-contrast scans\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pre-process: deskew, increase contrast\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reasoning about diagrams and UIs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact pixel coordinates and spatial relations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Verify coordinates; map them back to original size\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>VLMs also \u003cb>hallucinate\u003c/b>: they describe a plausible detail that is not in the image, or \"correct\" a number to what seems likely. And text inside images is an input channel: an image can carry instructions, which is an injection route (the Security module covers this).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Model choice.\u003c/b> Frontier VLMs from every major lab handle documents and screenshots well. Open-weight families (Qwen-VL, Gemma, Mistral and others) run on your own GPUs; small models such as SmolVLM and Moondream run on laptops and edge devices for narrow jobs like captioning or reading meters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Structured answers.\u003c/b> Ask for JSON with the value, the unit and where it was read (\"legend: 'EU', bar 3\"), which makes automatic checking possible.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Build a test set with known answers.\u003c/b> For charts, generate them from data you control, so ground truth is exact. For screenshots, write down the true field values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Re-ask to detect guessing.\u003c/b> Ask the same question twice in different words; inconsistent answers flag low confidence.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you ask a VLM questions about charts and screenshots and check its answers?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Build a small test set where the truth is known: charts generated from data you control (bar, line, pie) and screenshots whose field values you record. Send each image at a resolution where the text is legible, ask a specific question and request a structured answer (value, unit, where it was read), with an instruction to read labels before answering. Score automatically against ground truth, exact match for text and a tolerance (for example 2%) for values read from charts, then group the failures by cause (small text, dense charts, counting, colour legends) and re-test with higher resolution or cropping.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does LLaVA-1.5 produce 576 image tokens per image?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its CLIP ViT encoder takes 336 × 336-pixel images in 14 × 14-pixel patches, giving a 24 × 24 grid of 576 patches, each projected to one token for the LLM.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two tasks where you should not rely on a VLM alone, and what to do instead.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Exact counting of many small objects (use an object detection model or ask for an itemised list and verify) and precise coordinates or measurements (verify against the original image, or use a specialised model); also identifying people, which policies typically forbid.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:multimodal/image-tokens-and-cost":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Providers cut images into fixed-size \u003cb>patches\u003c/b> or \u003cb>tiles\u003c/b> and bill each image as input tokens. Cost grows with pixel area until a per-model cap, beyond which the image is downscaled. Most tasks need far less detail than the cap allows, so \u003cb>resizing and cropping\u003c/b> to what the task needs is the biggest lever on image cost and latency.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three counting schemes (as of late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every provider documents its own rule. They change with model generations, so read the current page, but the mechanisms below are stable.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Anthropic (Claude): 28-pixel patches.\u003c/b> Each 28 × 28-pixel block is one visual token:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>tokens = ceil(width / 28) × ceil(height / 28)\nCaps:  standard tier  1,568 px long edge, 1,568 tokens\n       high-res tier  2,576 px long edge, 4,784 tokens (newer models)\nLarger images are downscaled, keeping the aspect ratio, until both caps are met.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>OpenAI: 32-pixel patches with a budget and a multiplier.\u003c/b> For current models: count 32 × 32 patches, shrink the image if it exceeds the \u003cb>patch budget\u003c/b> of the chosen \u003ccode>detail\u003c/code> level (\u003ccode>low\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>high\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>original\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code>), then multiply by a per-model factor (1.2 for current flagship models). The docs' example: a 1024 × 1024 image is 32 × 32 = 1,024 patches, billed as ceil(1,024 × 1.2) = 1,229 tokens; a 2048 × 2048 image exceeds a 2,500-patch budget, is shrunk to 1600 × 1600 (2,500 patches) and billed 3,000 tokens. Older models used \u003cb>tiles\u003c/b>: 85 base tokens plus 170 per 512-pixel tile (GPT-4o), or a fixed small cost with \u003ccode>detail: low\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Google (Gemini): a resolution setting.\u003c/b> Gemini 3 and later models expose \u003ccode>media_resolution\u003c/code> (low, medium, high, ultra_high), which allocates up to roughly 280, 560, 1,120 or 2,240 tokens per image largely independent of its pixel size; the default is about 1,120. Older Gemini models charged 258 tokens for images up to 384 pixels in both dimensions and 258 per 768 × 768 tile above that. For PDFs, Google recommends \u003ccode>medium\u003c/code> because OCR quality typically saturates there.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked example: one screenshot, three providers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A 1920 × 1080 dashboard screenshot:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Claude, high-res tier:   ceil(1920/28) × ceil(1080/28) = 69 × 39 = 2,691 tokens\nClaude, standard tier:   downscaled to 1456 × 819      = 52 × 30 = 1,560 tokens\nOpenAI flagship, high:   ceil(1920/32) × ceil(1080/32) = 60 × 34 = 2,040 patches × 1.2 = 2,448 tokens\nGemini 3+, default:      up to about 1,120 tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Now the money. At an illustrative $3 per million input tokens, 2,691 tokens cost $0.0081 per image, or $807 for 100,000 screenshots a month. Resize to 1280 × 720 first:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Claude: ceil(1280/28) × ceil(720/28) = 46 × 26 = 1,196 tokens   (56% fewer)\n100,000 images × 1,196 tokens × $3 / 1,000,000 ≈ $359 a month\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Whether 1280 × 720 is enough depends on the smallest text the task must read. Test it: that is what the lab in this module does.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Resizing before you send\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from math import ceil\nfrom PIL import Image\n\ndef claude_tokens(w: int, h: int) -&gt; int:\n    return ceil(w / 28) * ceil(h / 28)\n\ndef prepare(path: str, max_long_edge: int = 1280) -&gt; Image.Image:\n    im = Image.open(path).convert(\"RGB\")\n    im.thumbnail((max_long_edge, max_long_edge))   # keeps aspect ratio, never enlarges\n    return im\n\nim = prepare(\"dashboard.png\")\nprint(im.size, claude_tokens(*im.size), \"tokens before any provider-side resizing\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Always confirm with the \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> object in the response: it reports the input tokens actually billed, images included.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Other levers\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Crop to the region of interest.\u003c/b> A 400 × 300 crop of the invoice total costs about 165 Claude tokens (15 × 11) instead of thousands.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use low detail for coarse tasks.\u003c/b> \"Is this a receipt or a selfie?\" does not need high resolution.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Colour does not matter.\u003c/b> Tokens depend on pixel dimensions, so converting to greyscale saves nothing; heavy JPEG compression shrinks the request but not the token count, and can damage text.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cache and batch.\u003c/b> Images in a stable prompt prefix are cached like text, and batch APIs discount image tokens like any other (the Billing module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Video and audio are tokens too.\u003c/b> Video is billed per sampled frame (for example roughly 70 tokens per frame at Gemini's low setting, 280 at high) and audio per second (Gemini counts about 25 to 32 tokens per second depending on the model generation).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Coordinates refer to the resized image.\u003c/b> If the model returns bounding boxes, map them back to the original dimensions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Estimate Claude's visual tokens for a 1000 × 1000 image and for an 800 × 600 image.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>1000 × 1000: ceil(1000/28) = 36, so 36 × 36 = 1,296 tokens. 800 × 600: ceil(800/28) = 29 and ceil(600/28) = 22, so 29 × 22 = 638 tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a 4K screenshot cost the same as a 1920 × 1080 one on some models?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Images above the model's long-edge or token cap are downscaled before tokenisation, so both end up at the same capped size and token count; on the Claude standard tier both become 1456 × 819 and 1,560 tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three ways to cut image cost without changing models.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Resize to the smallest resolution that keeps the needed text legible; crop to the relevant region; use a low detail or resolution setting for coarse tasks; and cache repeated images in the prompt prefix or use batch processing for offline jobs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:document-ai-ocr-layout-and-extraction":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Document AI\u003c/b> turns PDFs, scans and photos of forms into structured data. The pipeline has three jobs: \u003cb>get the text\u003c/b> (from the PDF's text layer, or with OCR), \u003cb>understand the layout\u003c/b> (blocks, reading order, tables) and \u003cb>extract fields\u003c/b> into a schema. In 2026 teams combine layout tools with VLMs and validate every field, because a confident wrong amount is worse than a missing one.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Born-digital or scanned?\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First check whether the PDF has a \u003cb>text layer\u003c/b>. Born-digital PDFs (exported from Word or a billing system) contain the exact characters; extracting them with a PDF library is fast, free and error-free at the character level. Scanned PDFs and phone photos are just images and need \u003cb>OCR\u003c/b> (optical character recognition). Many real files mix both: a digital contract with scanned signature pages. The RAG module covers parsing documents for retrieval; this lesson is about \u003cb>extracting fields\u003c/b> reliably.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>PDF or image\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Text layer or OCR\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Layout\u003c/b>\u003csmall>blocks, reading order\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Tables\u003c/b>\u003csmall>rows, columns, merged cells\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Extraction\u003c/b>\u003csmall>LLM or VLM to schema\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Validation\u003c/b>\u003csmall>rules, totals\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Human review queue\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>OCR\u003c/b> converts pixels to characters with positions and confidences. Classic engines (Tesseract, PaddleOCR) are free and fast; cloud services and VLM-based OCR are stronger on poor scans and handwriting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Layout analysis\u003c/b> finds headings, paragraphs, tables, figures and the \u003cb>reading order\u003c/b>, which matters for two-column pages and forms.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Table structure recognition\u003c/b> recovers rows, columns and merged cells. It is the hardest step and the most common source of silent errors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Field extraction\u003c/b> maps content to your schema (supplier, date, total, line items), usually with an LLM or VLM and structured outputs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Validation\u003c/b> checks business rules; low-confidence or failing documents go to a person.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Tools\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Good for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Docling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-source converter: layout, reading order, tables, OCR, exports to Markdown and JSON, optional VLM pipeline\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Self-hosted pipelines, RAG ingestion\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Marker, MinerU\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Open-source PDF to Markdown converters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bulk conversion, scientific documents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>olmOCR and other open OCR VLMs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small VLMs fine-tuned to linearise pages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High volume at low cost\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Azure Document Intelligence, AWS Textract, Google Document AI\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Managed OCR, layout, tables and prebuilt models for invoices, receipts, IDs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Enterprise forms with SLAs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Frontier VLM with structured outputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Send page images, get JSON\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Varied layouts, fast to build\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The olmOCR paper (early 2025) is a useful reference point for economics: it estimated converting a million PDF pages at about $176 with its open 7B model, against over $6,240 with a frontier API (GPT-4o) at the time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A minimal extraction pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom docling.document_converter import DocumentConverter\n\nclass LineItem(BaseModel):\n    description: str\n    quantity: float\n    unit_price: float\n    amount: float\n\nclass Invoice(BaseModel):\n    supplier_name: str\n    invoice_number: str\n    invoice_date: str          # ISO 8601, YYYY-MM-DD\n    currency: str\n    net: float\n    vat: float\n    total: float\n    line_items: list[LineItem]\n\nresult = DocumentConverter().convert(\"scanned_invoice.pdf\")      # layout, tables, OCR where needed\nmarkdown = result.document.export_to_markdown()\ntables = [t.export_to_dataframe(doc=result.document) for t in result.document.tables]\n# Send `markdown` (and, for hard pages, the page image) to an LLM with the Invoice schema as\n# a structured output (Tools module). Keep the page image for review and for VLM fallback.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Then validate before anything downstream sees the data:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def checks(inv: Invoice) -&gt; list[str]:\n    problems = []\n    if abs(sum(li.amount for li in inv.line_items) - inv.net) &gt; 0.01:\n        problems.append(\"line items do not sum to net\")\n    if abs(inv.net + inv.vat - inv.total) &gt; 0.01:\n        problems.append(\"net + vat != total\")\n    return problems   # non-empty: route to human review\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Arithmetic checks catch many OCR and VLM number errors for free, because a misread digit rarely keeps the sums consistent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Scoring field by field\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A document that is \"mostly right\" is useless if the total is wrong. Measure each field separately over a labelled set of 50 to 200 documents:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Label ground truth\u003c/b> by hand for every field, including line items.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Normalise\u003c/b> both sides: trim whitespace, unify date formats, strip currency symbols and thousands separators, casefold names.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compare\u003c/b> per field: exact match for identifiers and dates, a small tolerance for amounts, fuzzy match (character error rate) for free text.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tables\u003c/b>: align predicted rows to gold rows, then score cell by cell; report row precision and recall plus cell accuracy. Research uses \u003cb>TEDS\u003c/b> (tree-edit-distance similarity) for table structure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Report\u003c/b> per-field accuracy, not one average. An illustrative report:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>field            accuracy   most common error\nsupplier_name      98%      legal suffix dropped\ninvoice_date       96%      day and month swapped (US vs UK format)\ntotal              93%      thousands separator misread\nvat_number         85%      O read as 0\nline_items (cells) 81%      merged description cells split\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The table tells you exactly where to spend effort and which fields need human review before you automate.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tables spanning pages\u003c/b>, merged header cells and rotated pages break naive pipelines.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>VLMs \"fix\" numbers\u003c/b>: they may output a plausible total instead of the printed one. Cross-check against OCR text where you have it, and run arithmetic checks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Handwriting, stamps and checkboxes\u003c/b> need dedicated handling and testing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Personal data\u003c/b> is everywhere in documents; check where pages are sent and how long they are retained.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you extract tables from scanned PDFs and score them field by field?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Render or load the pages, run OCR plus layout and table-structure recognition (for example Docling with OCR enabled, a cloud document API or a VLM) and export each table to a dataframe or JSON with a fixed schema. Hand-label ground truth for 50 or more tables, normalise both sides (whitespace, number and date formats), align predicted rows to gold rows, then compute cell-level accuracy and row precision and recall, plus per-field accuracy for key columns such as amounts and dates with a numeric tolerance. Report the metrics per field with typical error examples, and add rule checks such as row totals summing to the stated total.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why check that line items sum to the net amount, if the model already extracted every number?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>OCR and VLM errors on single digits are common and silent; a misread digit almost always breaks the arithmetic, so the check catches errors without any ground truth and routes those documents to human review.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you skip OCR entirely?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the PDF is born-digital and has a text layer: extracting the embedded text is faster, cheaper and exact at the character level, though you may still need layout and table analysis.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:speech-to-text-with-whisper":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Speech-to-text\u003c/b> (automatic speech recognition, ASR) turns audio into text. Modern models such as OpenAI's \u003cb>Whisper\u003c/b> are encoder-decoder transformers trained on hundreds of thousands of hours of transcribed audio; you can run them yourself or call a hosted API. Quality is measured with \u003cb>word error rate (WER)\u003c/b> on \u003ci>your\u003c/i> audio after careful text normalisation, and production systems add streaming, speaker diarisation and custom vocabulary.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How Whisper works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Whisper (Radford et al., 2022) was trained on \u003cb>680,000 hours\u003c/b> of multilingual audio paired with transcripts collected from the web, a weakly supervised but enormous dataset, and approaches human robustness on many benchmarks without fine-tuning.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Audio\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>30-second window\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Log-Mel spectrogram\u003c/b>\u003csmall>frequencies over time\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Encoder\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Decoder\u003c/b>\u003csmall>text tokens + timestamps\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Transcript\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The audio is resampled to 16 kHz, cut into \u003cb>30-second windows\u003c/b> and turned into a \u003cb>log-Mel spectrogram\u003c/b>, an image-like grid of energy per frequency band over time. The encoder reads it; the decoder generates text like a language model, steered by special tokens for language, task (transcribe or translate to English) and timestamps. Because the decoder is a language model, Whisper produces fluent punctuation and casing, and, like any language model, can \u003cb>hallucinate\u003c/b>: a 2024 study (\"Careless Whisper\") found about 1% of transcriptions contained entire phrases that were never spoken, more often for speakers with long pauses. Running \u003cb>voice activity detection (VAD)\u003c/b> first, so silence is never transcribed, removes much of this.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\u003cth>Parameters\u003c/th>\u003cth>VRAM (approx.)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Speed vs large\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>tiny\u003c/td>\u003ctd>39M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~1 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~10×\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>small\u003c/td>\u003ctd>244M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~2 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~4×\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>large\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,550M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~10 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1×\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>turbo (optimised large-v3)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>809M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~6 GB\u003c/td>\u003ctd>~8×\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For self-hosting, \u003cb>faster-whisper\u003c/b> (a CTranslate2 reimplementation, up to 4 times faster for the same accuracy with less memory) and \u003cb>whisper.cpp\u003c/b> (CPU and Apple silicon) are the usual engines:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from faster_whisper import WhisperModel\n\nmodel = WhisperModel(\"turbo\", device=\"cuda\", compute_type=\"float16\")   # or device=\"cpu\", compute_type=\"int8\"\nsegments, info = model.transcribe(\"meeting.wav\", vad_filter=True, word_timestamps=True)\nprint(info.language, info.language_probability)\nfor s in segments:\n    print(f\"[{s.start:7.2f} -&gt; {s.end:7.2f}] {s.text}\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Hosted APIs (from OpenAI, Google, Deepgram, AssemblyAI, Speechmatics, ElevenLabs and others, as of late 2026) add streaming, diarisation and higher accuracy on hard audio; open-weight models keep audio in-house.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Word error rate, worked by hand\u003c/h4>\u003cp>WER counts the minimum number of word \u003cb>substitutions (S)\u003c/b>, \u003cb>deletions (D)\u003c/b> and \u003cb>insertions (I)\u003c/b> needed to turn the model's output into the reference, divided by the number of reference words \u003cb>(N)\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>WER = (S + D + I) / N\n\nReference:  please send the invoice to anna by friday           (N = 8)\nHypothesis: please send invoice to anne by friday ok\n\n\"the\" missing        -&gt; D = 1\n\"anna\" -&gt; \"anne\"     -&gt; S = 1\n\"ok\" extra           -&gt; I = 1\nWER = (1 + 1 + 1) / 8 = 0.375, i.e. 37.5%\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Because insertions count, WER can exceed 100%. In code:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import jiwer\n\nrefs = [\"please send the invoice to anna by friday\"]\nhyps = [\"please send invoice to anne by friday ok\"]\nout = jiwer.process_words(refs, hyps)\nprint(out.wer, out.substitutions, out.deletions, out.insertions)   # 0.375 1 1 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Measuring it properly\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Normalise first.\u003c/b> \"Dr. Smith paid $10\" and \"doctor smith paid ten dollars\" are the same speech. Lowercase, strip punctuation, and standardise numbers and abbreviations on \u003ci>both\u003c/i> sides with the same normaliser (the \u003ccode>whisper\u003c/code> package ships an \u003ccode>EnglishTextNormalizer\u003c/code>). Without it, WER mostly measures formatting.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Corpus WER, not the mean of file WERs.\u003c/b> Sum errors over all files and divide by total reference words; jiwer does this when you pass lists. Averaging per-file WERs over-weights short clips.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Slice the results\u003c/b> by speaker, accent, microphone, noise level and domain vocabulary. An overall 8% can hide 25% on one speaker.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use CER\u003c/b> (character error rate) for languages written without spaces, such as Chinese or Japanese.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Check what matters to the product.\u003c/b> For a booking line, names, dates and numbers matter more than filler words; score those entities separately.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Batch, streaming and diarisation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Batch\u003c/b> transcription of files is usually the most accurate and the cheapest (the model sees more context and there is no real-time constraint). \u003cb>Streaming\u003c/b> returns partial (interim) results within a few hundred milliseconds and revises them, then emits final segments; voice agents need it. \u003cb>Diarisation\u003c/b> answers \"who spoke when\", with open tools such as pyannote or as a hosted feature, and has its own metric (diarisation error rate). \u003cb>Custom vocabulary\u003c/b> or prompting with product names, people and jargon often gives large gains on business audio, where those words carry the meaning.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you transcribe meetings and measure word error rate?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run VAD plus a Whisper-family model (for example faster-whisper with \u003ccode>vad_filter=True\u003c/code>) or a hosted API with diarisation over the recordings. Hand-transcribe a reference for a representative sample, for example ten 5-minute clips covering different speakers and rooms. Normalise both references and hypotheses with the same normaliser (case, punctuation, numbers), compute corpus-level WER = (S + D + I) / N with a tool such as jiwer, and report it overall and sliced by speaker and condition, plus the accuracy of names and numbers that matter to the business.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Reference \"book a table for two at seven\" (7 words); hypothesis \"book table for two at seven pm\". What is the WER?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One deletion (\"a\") and one insertion (\"pm\"), no substitutions, so WER = 2 / 7 ≈ 28.6%.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does running VAD before Whisper reduce hallucinations?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Whisper's decoder is a language model that tends to produce plausible text even when the input is silence or noise; VAD removes non-speech segments so the model is never asked to transcribe them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:multimodal/text-to-speech":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Text-to-speech (TTS)\u003c/b> turns text into audio. Modern systems either predict a spectrogram and render it with a \u003cb>vocoder\u003c/b>, or generate discrete \u003cb>audio codec tokens\u003c/b> with a language-model-style network. For a product, what matters is \u003cb>time to first audio\u003c/b>, streaming support, naturalness, pronunciation control, voice consistency, cost and the ethics of voices.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Text\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Normalise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>numbers, dates, symbols\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Phonemes\u003c/b>\u003csmall>how words sound\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Acoustic model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>spectrogram or codec tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Vocoder or codec decoder\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Audio\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Text normalisation\u003c/b> turns \"Dr. Lee, 3/4 at 14:30, $4.50\" into words. Errors here are among the most common TTS bugs in production (\"three quarters\" instead of \"the third of April\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Grapheme-to-phoneme (G2P)\u003c/b> conversion maps spelling to sounds, using a dictionary plus a model for unknown words. Names and brand terms often need overrides.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An \u003cb>acoustic model\u003c/b> predicts what the speech should sound like, with prosody: rhythm, stress, intonation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \u003cb>vocoder\u003c/b> (or the decoder of a neural audio codec) produces the waveform, typically at 16, 24 or 48 kHz.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The newer codec-token approach treats audio like text: a neural codec compresses audio into tokens, and a transformer predicts those tokens. It enables \u003cb>zero-shot voice cloning\u003c/b> from a few seconds of reference audio and expressive, steerable speech, and it is the same machinery inside speech-to-speech models (next lesson).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Streaming and latency\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In a voice agent, users hear nothing until the first audio arrives, so \u003cb>time to first byte (TTFB)\u003c/b> matters more than total synthesis time. Three techniques:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stream the output.\u003c/b> Most TTS APIs send audio in chunks as it is generated (OpenAI's uses chunked transfer encoding), so playback starts before synthesis ends.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stream the input.\u003c/b> Do not wait for the LLM's full reply. Send the first sentence or clause to TTS as soon as it is complete; some providers accept a text stream over a WebSocket.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pick a cheap format.\u003c/b> Raw \u003ccode>pcm\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>wav\u003c/code> avoids encoding delay (OpenAI's docs recommend them for the fastest responses; its \u003ccode>pcm\u003c/code> is 24 kHz 16-bit little-endian samples); \u003ccode>opus\u003c/code> suits network streaming; \u003ccode>mp3\u003c/code> is the usual file default. Telephone lines use 8 kHz G.711 (mu-law in North America and Japan, A-law elsewhere), so resample for phone calls.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nwith client.audio.speech.with_streaming_response.create(\n    model=\"&lt;tts-model&gt;\",\n    voice=\"&lt;voice&gt;\",\n    input=\"Your appointment is confirmed for Tuesday the 4th of March at half past two.\",\n    instructions=\"Warm and calm, moderate pace.\",   # style steering, on models that support it\n    response_format=\"pcm\",\n) as response:\n    for chunk in response.iter_bytes():\n        player.write(chunk)                          # your audio output, started on the first chunk\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Running locally, an open model such as Kokoro (82 million parameters, Apache 2.0) generates 24 kHz audio on a CPU or small GPU:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from kokoro import KPipeline\nimport soundfile as sf\n\ntts = KPipeline(lang_code=\"a\")                       # \"a\" = American English\ntext = \"Your order from [Kokoro](/kˈOkəɹO/) ships today.\"   # inline phonemes fix one word's pronunciation\nfor i, (graphemes, phonemes, audio) in enumerate(tts(text, voice=\"af_heart\")):\n    sf.write(f\"part{i}.wav\", audio, 24000)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Controlling what it says and how\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Normalise yourself\u003c/b> before sending text: expand dates, currencies, units and codes into the words you want spoken. It is deterministic and testable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pronunciation overrides\u003c/b>: phoneme markup (as in Kokoro's syntax), SSML \u003ccode>&lt;phoneme&gt;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>&lt;say-as&gt;\u003c/code> tags on providers that support SSML, or pronunciation dictionaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Style\u003c/b>: SSML prosody tags, or natural-language instructions on newer models.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Write for the ear.\u003c/b> An LLM's reply for voice should be short, without lists, markdown or URLs. Put this in the voice prompt.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Evaluating TTS\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Naturalness\u003c/b>: \u003cb>MOS\u003c/b> (mean opinion score), people rating samples from 1 to 5, or side-by-side preference tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Intelligibility\u003c/b>: transcribe the TTS output with an ASR model and compute WER against the input text; spikes reveal mispronounced or skipped words.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pronunciation test set\u003c/b>: your product names, people, addresses, numbers and dates, checked by ear before every voice or model change.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Latency\u003c/b>: TTFB at p50 and p95 under load.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Ethics and law\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Tell people they are hearing an AI voice: OpenAI's usage policies, for example, require a clear disclosure to end users that the TTS voice is AI-generated, and several jurisdictions require disclosure for automated calls. Clone a real person's voice only with their documented consent. Providers increasingly watermark generated audio.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Providers to know (late 2026): ElevenLabs, Cartesia, OpenAI, Google, Microsoft Azure and Amazon Polly for hosted voices; Kokoro, Piper and other open models for self-hosting.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does time to first audio matter more than total synthesis time in a voice agent?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The user hears silence until the first audio plays, and the rest is generated faster than it is spoken while playback continues, so perceived responsiveness depends almost entirely on how quickly the first chunk arrives.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How can you test TTS intelligibility automatically?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Synthesise a test set of sentences, transcribe the audio with a good ASR model and compute WER against the input text; high WER on specific sentences points to mispronounced, skipped or garbled words.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two ways to fix a TTS system that reads \"3/4\" as \"three quarters\" in appointment confirmations.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Normalise the text before TTS (render dates as \"the third of April\"), or use SSML say-as or a pronunciation rule where the provider supports it; also make the LLM write dates in words in voice replies.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:realtime-speech-to-speech-apis":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A voice agent either \u003cb>chains\u003c/b> speech-to-text, a text LLM and text-to-speech (\u003cb>cascaded\u003c/b>), or uses one \u003cb>speech-to-speech\u003c/b> model that hears audio and speaks audio directly. Cascaded gives control, choice and inspectable text; speech-to-speech gives lower latency and more natural conversation. A third design is emerging: \u003cb>full-duplex\u003c/b> voice models that talk while a separate backend agent does the reasoning and tool work. Whatever you choose, budget latency per stage, because people notice gaps longer than about a second.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Cascaded pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Mic\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>VAD and turn detection\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Streaming STT\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Text LLM + tools\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Streaming TTS\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Speaker\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pros:\u003c/b> pick the best component for each stage; any LLM, including your existing text agent with its tools and RAG; the text in the middle can be logged, checked by guardrails and evaluated with ordinary text evals; easy to swap vendors. \u003cb>Cons:\u003c/b> latencies add up; tone, emotion and hesitation in the user's voice are lost in transcription; turn-taking is rigid because each stage waits for the previous one. Frameworks such as Pipecat and LiveKit Agents (next lesson) exist mainly to stream these stages into each other.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Speech-to-speech\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One model takes audio tokens in and produces audio tokens out, with text transcripts on the side. As of late 2026 the main hosted options are OpenAI's \u003cb>Realtime API\u003c/b> and Google's \u003cb>Gemini Live API\u003c/b> (the Live multimodal APIs lesson). \u003cb>Pros:\u003c/b> lower latency (no hand-offs), expressive speech that reacts to the user's tone, built-in voice activity detection and interruption handling. \u003cb>Cons:\u003c/b> fewer model choices, often a higher price per minute, harder to inspect and guard (the \"text in the middle\" is only a transcript), and the model's reasoning and tool use may lag the best text models.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Full-duplex and delegation\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Humans listen while speaking: they backchannel (\"mm-hm\"), get interrupted, overlap. \u003cb>Moshi\u003c/b> (Kyutai, 2024) showed a full-duplex research model that models its own speech and the user's in parallel streams, with a theoretical latency of 160 ms and about 200 ms in practice. Products are following: by late 2026 OpenAI offers \u003cb>GPT-Live\u003c/b>, a full-duplex voice front end that keeps the conversation going while it \u003cb>delegates\u003c/b> reasoning and tool calls to a separate backend (your own agent, or a hosted model). This splits the job sensibly: a fast voice model for talking, a strong text agent for thinking.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The latency budget\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Voice-to-voice latency runs from the moment the user stops speaking to the moment they hear the first audio. Studies of conversation across many languages find typical gaps between turns of around 200 ms; voice agents usually target well under a second. An illustrative cascaded budget (measure your own):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Stage\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical range\u003c/th>\u003cth>Main levers\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Network and audio capture in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>20-100 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>WebRTC, nearby region\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>End-of-turn detection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>200-800 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Semantic turn detection, tuned silence threshold\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Final transcript after end of speech\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100-300 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Streaming STT\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>LLM time to first token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>200-800 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Smaller model, cached prompt prefix, short context\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Waiting for the first clause\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50-300 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Send the first clause to TTS immediately\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>TTS time to first audio\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100-300 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Streaming TTS, pcm output\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Network out and playback buffer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>50-150 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>WebRTC, small jitter buffer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Total\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>about 0.7-2.7 s\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Tool calls add their own time: a 1.5-second database lookup blows the budget, so the agent should say something short (\"Let me check that\") while it waits. A speech-to-speech model collapses the STT, LLM and TTS rows into one model's time to first audio, but end-of-turn detection and the network remain.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Measuring response latency\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio, base64, json, os, time\nimport websockets\n\nURL = \"wss://api.openai.com/v1/realtime?model=&lt;realtime-model&gt;\"\nAPI_KEY = os.environ[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]\nHEADERS = {\"Authorization\": \"Bearer \" + API_KEY}\n\nasync def measure(pcm_chunks: list[bytes]) -&gt; None:      # sketch; 24 kHz 16-bit mono, 40 ms chunks\n    async with websockets.connect(URL, additional_headers=HEADERS) as ws:\n        await ws.send(json.dumps({\"type\": \"session.update\", \"session\": {\n            \"type\": \"realtime\",\n            \"instructions\": \"You are a concise booking assistant.\",\n            \"audio\": {\"input\": {\"turn_detection\": {\"type\": \"server_vad\", \"silence_duration_ms\": 500}}},\n        }}))\n\n        async def stream_mic():\n            for chunk in pcm_chunks:\n                await ws.send(json.dumps({\"type\": \"input_audio_buffer.append\",\n                                          \"audio\": base64.b64encode(chunk).decode()}))\n                await asyncio.sleep(0.04)                 # pace like a real microphone\n\n        sender = asyncio.create_task(stream_mic())\n        stopped = None\n        async for raw in ws:\n            event = json.loads(raw)\n            if event[\"type\"] == \"input_audio_buffer.speech_stopped\":\n                stopped = time.perf_counter()\n            elif event[\"type\"] == \"response.output_audio.delta\" and stopped:\n                print(f\"speech stopped -&gt; first audio: {(time.perf_counter() - stopped) * 1000:.0f} ms\")\n                stopped = None\n            elif event[\"type\"] == \"response.done\":\n                break\n        await sender\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Note what this measures: the server emits \u003ccode>speech_stopped\u003c/code> only after the configured 500 ms of silence, so the user waited that long \u003ci>plus\u003c/i> the printed number. Measure on the client too, from the real end of speech to audio actually playing, and report p50 and p95 over at least 20 turns of realistic audio.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Transport\u003c/b>: WebRTC for browsers and mobile (handles jitter and echo cancellation), WebSockets for server-to-server, SIP or telephony providers for phone lines. Browsers should connect with short-lived tokens minted by your server, never your API key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decide on control versus naturalness\u003c/b>: regulated workflows that need text guardrails and audit logs lean cascaded or delegated; consumer conversation leans speech-to-speech.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate in stages\u003c/b>: OpenAI's guide suggests crawl (synthetic speech, single turns), walk (recorded human audio), run (simulated multi-turn callers), measuring task success, audible latency, unwanted silence and interruptions separately.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you build a realtime voice session and measure its response latency?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Open a session with a speech-to-speech API (WebRTC from the browser with a short-lived token, or a server-side WebSocket), configure it with \u003ccode>session.update\u003c/code> (instructions, voice, turn detection), stream microphone audio in small PCM chunks (\u003ccode>input_audio_buffer.append\u003c/code>) and play the returned audio deltas. Record timestamps for the end of user speech (the \u003ccode>speech_stopped\u003c/code> event, remembering it fires after the silence threshold, and ideally your own client-side VAD) and for the first audio delta played, then report p50 and p95 voice-to-voice latency over 20 or more realistic turns, broken down by stage where possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give two reasons to choose a cascaded pipeline over speech-to-speech.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any reasons such as: you need text in the middle for guardrails, logging, audit or text evals; you want to reuse an existing text agent with its tools and RAG; you want to choose or swap the best STT, LLM and TTS vendors independently; or you need the strongest reasoning model.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your cascaded agent averages 1.9 s voice-to-voice, with 700 ms of end-of-turn silence and 650 ms LLM time to first token. What do you change first?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Attack the two largest stages: replace the fixed 700 ms silence wait with semantic turn detection or a shorter tuned threshold, and cut time to first token with a smaller or faster model, a cached prompt prefix and a shorter context; then stream the first clause straight into TTS.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:voice-agent-frameworks-pipecat-and-livekit-agent":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Turn detection\u003c/b> decides when the user has finished, so the agent can reply; \u003cb>interruption handling\u003c/b> (barge-in) decides when the user wants the agent to stop. Silence-based \u003cb>voice activity detection (VAD)\u003c/b> alone either cuts people off mid-thought or makes the agent sluggish, so modern agents add a model that judges whether the utterance sounds \u003ci>complete\u003c/i>. When the user interrupts, the agent must stop audio within a fraction of a second, cancel generation and \u003cb>truncate its own history\u003c/b> to what the user actually heard.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>VAD and endpointing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A VAD model such as \u003cb>Silero VAD\u003c/b> scores short audio frames (tens of milliseconds) for speech probability. \u003cb>Endpointing\u003c/b> turns that into a decision: \"the user has been silent for N milliseconds, so the turn is over\". OpenAI's Realtime API exposes the classic knobs in its \u003ccode>server_vad\u003c/code> mode, with this documented example: \u003ccode>threshold\u003c/code> 0.5 (how loud speech must be), \u003ccode>prefix_padding_ms\u003c/code> 300 (audio kept from before speech started) and \u003ccode>silence_duration_ms\u003c/code> 500 (silence that ends the turn).\u003c/p>\u003cp>The silence threshold is a painful trade-off:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Silence threshold\u003c/th>\u003cth>What happens\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>200-300 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Snappy, but cuts off \"My account number is... (pause) ...4 7 1 2\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>500-700 ms\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Common default; still interrupts slow speakers and people reading numbers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1,000 ms or more\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rarely cuts people off, but every reply feels slow\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Semantic and audio turn detection\u003c/h4>\u003cp>People signal the end of a turn with grammar, intonation and pace, not only silence. Three production approaches:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Semantic VAD\u003c/b> in OpenAI's Realtime API (\u003ccode>\"type\": \"semantic_vad\"\u003c/code>) scores how likely the user's words are complete: it waits longer after \"I'd like to book a table for...\" than after \"That's all, thanks.\" An \u003ccode>eagerness\u003c/code> setting (low, medium, high, auto) tunes how quickly it responds.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>LiveKit's turn detector model\u003c/b>, the default in its \u003ccode>AgentSession\u003c/code>, predicts end of turn from both the meaning and the acoustics of speech, on top of VAD. Endpointing delays (\u003ccode>min_delay\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_delay\u003c/code>, or dynamic endpointing that adapts to the speaker's pauses) bound the wait.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Smart Turn\u003c/b> (from the Pipecat project) is an open, BSD-licensed, audio-native model: it listens to the raw audio of the last utterance, including prosody, supports 23 languages and runs in as little as 10 ms on some CPUs (under 100 ms on most cloud instances). It only runs when VAD detects a pause, so it is cheap.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Interruptions (barge-in)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User speaks while agent talks\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Filter\u003c/b>\u003csmall>backchannel or real interruption?\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Stop playback now\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Cancel LLM and TTS\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Truncate history to heard text\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Handle new turn\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Detect.\u003c/b> User speech starts while the agent is speaking.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Filter backchannels.\u003c/b> \"Mm-hm\", \"yeah\", a cough or the TV are not interruptions. Require a minimum duration or number of words (LiveKit's \u003ccode>min_duration\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>min_words\u003c/code>), or use an adaptive detector that distinguishes backchannels from real interruptions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stop fast.\u003c/b> Halt playback within about 100 to 200 ms and drop queued audio; a few seconds of the agent talking over the user is one of the most irritating failures in voice UX.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cancel work.\u003c/b> Stop the LLM and TTS streams so you do not pay for, or later play, audio nobody will hear.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Truncate history.\u003c/b> The agent's last message must contain only what was played. If the agent planned \"Your total is 240 euros, and I've also added insurance\" but was cut off after \"240 euros\", the history must not claim the user heard about insurance. OpenAI's Realtime API does this server-side for WebRTC and SIP connections; over WebSockets your client sends \u003ccode>conversation.item.truncate\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>audio_end_ms\u003c/code>, the amount actually played. Gemini's Live API cancels generation on interruption and keeps in history only what was already sent to the client, so a client that buffers audio should keep its buffer short.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Handle false interruptions.\u003c/b> If the \"interruption\" turns out to be noise with no words, resume. LiveKit's documented example resumes after 2 seconds of silence (\u003ccode>false_interruption_timeout\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>resume_false_interruption\u003c/code>).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>\u003cb>Echo\u003c/b> is the other classic bug: on a speakerphone the agent hears its own voice and interrupts itself. WebRTC's acoustic echo cancellation handles most cases; telephony and custom audio paths need it explicitly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Frameworks: Pipecat and LiveKit Agents\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Both are open-source Python frameworks (with other SDKs) that stream audio through STT, LLM and TTS stages, or through a speech-to-speech model, with VAD, turn detection, interruptions and transports built in.\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pipecat\u003c/b> (BSD-2 licence) models an agent as a \u003cb>pipeline of processors\u003c/b> that pass frames (audio, text, control signals) to each other, with integrations for well over a hundred AI services and transports for WebRTC, WebSockets and telephony.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>LiveKit Agents\u003c/b> runs agents as participants in LiveKit's WebRTC rooms, with an \u003ccode>AgentSession\u003c/code> that wires STT, LLM, TTS, VAD and its turn detector, plus telephony.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>A LiveKit configuration sketch, following its docs as of late 2026 (check the current reference before copying):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from livekit.agents import AgentSession, TurnHandlingOptions, inference\n\nsession = AgentSession(\n    turn_handling=TurnHandlingOptions(\n        turn_detection=inference.TurnDetector(),          # meaning + acoustics, on top of VAD\n        interruption={\"min_words\": 2,                     # ignore \"mm-hm\" and coughs\n                      \"false_interruption_timeout\": 2.0,\n                      \"resume_false_interruption\": True},\n    ),\n    # stt=..., llm=..., tts=..., vad=...\n)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Testing turn-taking\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Script and record test calls for the hard cases, and replay them after every change: a long pause mid-sentence; reading a card or phone number slowly; backchannels while the agent speaks; a real interruption with a correction (\"no, Thursday\"); background noise and a second voice; speakerphone echo. Measure false cut-offs, missed interruptions, time to stop speaking and unwanted silence.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you build a voice agent with turn detection and interruptions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Use a framework such as Pipecat or LiveKit Agents (or a speech-to-speech API with built-in VAD) over a WebRTC or telephony transport. Run a VAD such as Silero plus a semantic or audio turn-detection model (LiveKit's turn detector, Smart Turn or OpenAI's semantic VAD) with tuned endpointing delays. Enable interruptions: when the user speaks during agent speech and passes a backchannel filter (minimum words or duration), stop playback within about 200 ms, cancel LLM and TTS generation, truncate the agent's last message to the audio actually played, and process the new user turn; resume after false interruptions, enable echo cancellation, and test with scripted calls for pauses, numbers, backchannels and corrections.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the agent's history be truncated after an interruption?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Otherwise the model believes the user heard the whole planned reply, and later turns refer to information, offers or confirmations the user never heard, causing confusion or wrong actions.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A user reading a 16-digit card number keeps getting cut off. What do you change?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Lengthen or adapt endpointing for this state (a longer silence threshold or lower eagerness while collecting digits), use a semantic or audio turn detector that recognises an incomplete number, or switch to an explicit input mode such as keypad entry for card numbers.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:live-multimodal-apis":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>live API\u003c/b> keeps a two-way stream open: you push microphone audio continuously (and, with some models, camera or screen frames), and the model streams speech back with built-in VAD, interruptions, transcripts and tool calls. It behaves like a phone call rather than a request: you manage a \u003cb>session\u003c/b> with time limits, a growing context, reconnection and short-lived credentials for client devices.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The session model\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Client\u003c/b>\u003csmall>mic + camera\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Your server or ephemeral token\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Live session\u003c/b>\u003csmall>WebSocket or WebRTC\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>hears, sees, speaks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Events\u003c/b>\u003csmall>audio, transcripts, tool calls, interrupted\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Everything is an \u003cb>event\u003c/b>. Typical client events: configure the session, append audio, send an image frame, return a tool result. Typical server events: audio chunks, input and output transcripts, \"user started or stopped speaking\", \"generation interrupted\", tool calls, turn complete.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>As of late 2026\u003c/th>\u003cth>Gemini Live API\u003c/th>\u003cth>OpenAI Realtime API\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Transport\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stateful WebSocket\u003c/td>\u003ctd>WebRTC, WebSocket, SIP\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Audio in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raw 16-bit PCM, 16 kHz, little-endian\u003c/td>\u003ctd>PCM at 24 kHz (also G.711 for telephony)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Audio out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raw 16-bit PCM, 24 kHz\u003c/td>\u003ctd>PCM at 24 kHz\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Visual input\u003c/td>\u003ctd>JPEG frames, at most 1 per second\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Images as message content\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Turn detection\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Automatic VAD with sensitivity and silence settings\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>server_vad\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>semantic_vad\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Client credentials\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ephemeral tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ephemeral client secrets\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Streaming audio and video (Gemini, Python)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A sketch based on the google-genai SDK's documented calls; \u003ccode>mic_chunks\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>screen_frames\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>player\u003c/code> stand in for your audio capture, frame grabber and playback:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import asyncio\nfrom google import genai\nfrom google.genai import types\n\nclient = genai.Client()                                   # reads the API key from the environment\nCONFIG = {\n    \"response_modalities\": [\"AUDIO\"],\n    \"system_instruction\": \"You help users with what is on their screen. Be brief.\",\n    \"input_audio_transcription\": {},\n    \"output_audio_transcription\": {},\n}\n\nasync def run(mic_chunks, screen_frames, player):\n    async with client.aio.live.connect(model=\"&lt;live-model&gt;\", config=CONFIG) as session:\n        async def send_audio():\n            async for chunk in mic_chunks():              # 16-bit PCM, 16 kHz mono, 20-100 ms per chunk\n                await session.send_realtime_input(\n                    audio=types.Blob(data=chunk, mime_type=\"audio/pcm;rate=16000\"))\n\n        async def send_video():\n            async for jpeg in screen_frames():            # at most one frame per second\n                await session.send_realtime_input(\n                    video=types.Blob(data=jpeg, mime_type=\"image/jpeg\"))\n\n        senders = [asyncio.create_task(send_audio()), asyncio.create_task(send_video())]\n        while True:\n            async for msg in session.receive():           # one model turn at a time\n                sc = msg.server_content\n                if not sc:\n                    continue\n                if sc.interrupted:\n                    player.clear()                        # user barged in: drop queued audio\n                if sc.model_turn:\n                    for part in sc.model_turn.parts:\n                        if part.inline_data:\n                            player.write(part.inline_data.data)   # 24 kHz PCM\n                if sc.input_transcription:\n                    print(\"user:\", sc.input_transcription.text)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Video and screen input\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Frames are images, so each costs image tokens (the Image tokens lesson). At one frame per second a 10-minute screen-share session sends 600 frames. Practical rules: send frames only when the screen changes or when the user asks about it; downscale to the smallest size where text stays legible; never stream screens or cameras without clear user consent and a visible indicator.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Limits, context and reconnection\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Live sessions accumulate every audio chunk and frame in context, so they hit limits that ordinary requests never meet. Gemini's documented limits (late 2026): without compression, audio-only sessions last up to 15 minutes and audio-plus-video sessions up to 2 minutes; a single connection lasts around 10 minutes. The tools to handle this:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Context window compression\u003c/b> (a sliding window over older turns) extends sessions beyond those limits.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Session resumption\u003c/b>: the server issues resumption tokens (valid for 2 hours after the session ends), so a client can reconnect without losing the conversation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>GoAway messages\u003c/b> warn that a connection is about to close, giving you time to reconnect cleanly.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Cost also grows with session length: every turn is processed against an ever longer audio context, so long sessions need compression or periodic summarisation, exactly as text conversations do (the Memory module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Security\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Streaming straight from the browser to the provider (client-to-server) gives the lowest latency, but \u003cb>never ship your API key to the client\u003c/b>. Your server mints a short-lived ephemeral token or client secret with a restricted configuration, and the client uses that. Tools the live model can call should run on your server with the user's permissions, and spoken requests for sensitive actions deserve the same confirmation steps as typed ones.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you stream audio and video to a live model session?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Open a live session (for Gemini, a WebSocket via \u003ccode>client.aio.live.connect\u003c/code> with a config setting audio responses, system instructions and transcription), then concurrently send microphone audio as small raw 16-bit PCM chunks at 16 kHz and camera or screen frames as JPEG images at no more than one per second with \u003ccode>send_realtime_input\u003c/code>, while a receive loop plays the 24 kHz audio replies, clears queued audio on \u003ccode>interrupted\u003c/code>, logs transcripts and answers tool calls. Use ephemeral tokens for browser clients, and manage limits with context window compression, session resumption tokens and GoAway messages.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do live sessions need context compression even if each user turn is short?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every audio chunk and video frame stays in the session's context, so it grows continuously; without compression the session hits its duration or context limits (on Gemini, 15 minutes for audio, 2 minutes with video) and each turn becomes more expensive.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why use ephemeral tokens instead of an API key in a browser voice app?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Anything shipped to the browser can be extracted; a short-lived, narrowly scoped token limits what an attacker can do and for how long, while the real key stays on your server.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:image-generation-and-editing-with-diffusion":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>diffusion model\u003c/b> generates an image by starting from random noise and removing noise step by step, guided by a text prompt, until an image remains. \u003cb>Latent diffusion\u003c/b> does this in a small compressed space, which made it affordable; current models use \u003cb>transformers\u003c/b> and \u003cb>flow matching\u003c/b> for better quality in fewer steps. Editing reuses the same process: re-noise an existing image partway (\u003cb>image-to-image\u003c/b>), repaint only a masked region (\u003cb>inpainting\u003c/b>), or follow a written instruction (\u003cb>instruction editing\u003c/b>).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How diffusion works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Training:\u003c/b> take a real image, add a random amount of Gaussian noise, and train a network to predict the noise that was added, given the noisy image, the noise level and the caption. \u003cb>Generation:\u003c/b> start from pure noise and repeatedly subtract the predicted noise, typically over 20 to 50 steps (or 1 to 4 for distilled models).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Prompt\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Text encoder\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Denoiser\u003c/b>\u003csmall>U-Net or transformer, repeated N steps\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Latent\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>VAE decoder\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Image\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three ideas make it practical:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Latent space.\u003c/b> A variational autoencoder (VAE) compresses the image first. In Stable Diffusion-style models a 1024 × 1024 × 3 image becomes a 128 × 128 × 4 latent, 48 times fewer numbers, and the denoiser works there. The VAE decoder turns the final latent back into pixels.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Text conditioning.\u003c/b> A text encoder (CLIP, T5 or an LLM) embeds the prompt, and the denoiser attends to those embeddings at every step.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Classifier-free guidance (CFG).\u003c/b> At each step the model predicts noise twice, with and without the prompt, and pushes the result further in the prompt's direction: \u003ccode>noise = uncond + scale × (cond − uncond)\u003c/code>. A guidance scale around 4 to 8 makes images follow the prompt more closely at some cost in variety; distilled \"turbo\" models are trained to work with guidance off (scale 0).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Newer models (Stable Diffusion 3, FLUX, Qwen-Image) replace the U-Net with a \u003cb>diffusion transformer\u003c/b> and train with \u003cb>rectified flow\u003c/b> (flow matching), which learns a straighter path from noise to image, so fewer steps are needed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The controls\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Control\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Prompt and negative prompt\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What to draw, and what to steer away from\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Steps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>More steps, more refinement, more time (distilled models need 1 to 4)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Guidance scale\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How strongly to follow the prompt\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Seed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fixes the starting noise, so results are reproducible\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strength (image-to-image)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How much of the input to destroy: 0.3 keeps composition, 0.8 mostly redraws\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Mask (inpainting)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>White pixels are repainted, black pixels kept\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>ControlNet, LoRA\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Condition on edges, pose or depth; add a trained style or subject\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Generating and editing with Diffusers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import torch\nfrom diffusers import AutoPipelineForText2Image, AutoPipelineForImage2Image\n\nt2i = AutoPipelineForText2Image.from_pretrained(\n    \"stabilityai/sdxl-turbo\", torch_dtype=torch.float16, variant=\"fp16\").to(\"cuda\")\nseed = torch.Generator(\"cuda\").manual_seed(42)              # same seed + settings = same image\n\nimage = t2i(\"isometric illustration of a small data centre, pastel colours\",\n            num_inference_steps=1, guidance_scale=0.0, generator=seed).images[0]\n\ni2i = AutoPipelineForImage2Image.from_pipe(t2i)             # reuse the loaded weights\nnight = i2i(\"the same data centre at night, neon lights\", image=image.resize((512, 512)),\n            num_inference_steps=2, strength=0.5, guidance_scale=0.0, generator=seed).images[0]\nnight.save(\"night.png\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>SDXL-Turbo is distilled for 1 to 4 steps at 512 × 512; for image-to-image its model card notes that \u003ccode>num_inference_steps × strength\u003c/code> must be at least 1. \u003cb>Inpainting\u003c/b> uses \u003ccode>AutoPipelineForInpainting\u003c/code> with an inpainting checkpoint and a \u003ccode>mask_image\u003c/code>. \u003cb>Instruction editing\u003c/b> models take an image and an instruction directly; for example, with Qwen-Image-Edit (Apache 2.0, a large model that needs a data-centre GPU or quantisation):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from PIL import Image\nfrom diffusers import QwenImageEditPipeline\n\nphoto = Image.open(\"shopfront.jpg\").convert(\"RGB\")\nedit = QwenImageEditPipeline.from_pretrained(\"Qwen/Qwen-Image-Edit\", torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16).to(\"cuda\")\nout = edit(image=photo, prompt=\"Change the shop sign to say OPEN\", negative_prompt=\" \",\n           true_cfg_scale=4.0, num_inference_steps=50, generator=seed).images[0]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Hosted image models\u003c/h4>\u003cp>As of late 2026, frontier image generation and editing is offered by OpenAI (GPT Image models), Google (Gemini image models), Black Forest Labs (FLUX) and others. The clear trend is \u003cb>conversational editing\u003c/b>: generate, then refine over several turns (\"make the background darker\", \"keep the logo, change the font\") inside a multimodal model, alongside classic mask-based edits.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Licences differ by model.\u003c/b> FLUX.1 [schnell] and Qwen-Image are Apache 2.0; SDXL-Turbo uses a Stability AI non-commercial community licence with separate commercial terms. Check before shipping.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Provenance.\u003c/b> Google marks all Gemini-generated images with a SynthID watermark, and OpenAI's provenance tooling checks C2PA content credentials and SynthID in images. Metadata such as C2PA can be stripped by re-encoding; watermarks embedded in pixels survive more transformations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Safety and rights.\u003c/b> Hosted APIs moderate prompts and outputs; self-hosted models need your own filters. Avoid real people's likenesses and trademarked characters without rights.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate with people.\u003c/b> Automatic scores (prompt adherence judged by a VLM, aesthetic models) help triage, but side-by-side human preference on your use case decides.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost and time.\u003c/b> Seconds to tens of seconds per image on a GPU, with cost scaling with resolution and steps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you generate and edit images with an open diffusion model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Load a pipeline with Hugging Face Diffusers (for example SDXL-Turbo or FLUX.1 [schnell] for text to image), fix a seed and generate from a prompt with the model's recommended steps and guidance scale; edit by passing an existing image to an image-to-image pipeline with a \u003ccode>strength\u003c/code> (lower keeps more of the original), by inpainting with a mask where white pixels are repainted, or with an instruction-editing model such as Qwen-Image-Edit; check the model's licence before commercial use.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does classifier-free guidance do, and why do turbo models set it to 0?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It runs the denoiser with and without the prompt and amplifies the difference, making images follow the prompt more strongly; turbo models are distilled to produce good images in 1 to 4 steps without it, so guidance is switched off.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does diffusing in latent space make generation cheaper?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The VAE compresses the image (for example 1024 × 1024 × 3 to 128 × 128 × 4, about 48 times fewer values), so every denoising step processes far less data, and only the final latent is decoded back to pixels.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:multimodal/video-generation-and-world-models":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Video models extend image diffusion to \u003cb>time\u003c/b>: a video autoencoder compresses clips in space and time, and a diffusion transformer denoises all frames together, often generating synchronised audio too. \u003cb>World models\u003c/b> go further and generate an interactive environment frame by frame in real time, reacting to actions. Both are improving fast and their APIs change faster still, so isolate them behind an interface.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How video generation works\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The recipe mirrors latent image diffusion. A \u003cb>video VAE\u003c/b> compresses the clip; Wan2.2's (an open-weight family from Alibaba, Apache 2.0, released in 2025) compresses by 16 × 16 in space and 4 × in time. The latent is cut into \u003cb>spacetime patches\u003c/b>, small cubes covering a few pixels over a few frames, which a \u003cb>diffusion transformer\u003c/b> denoises jointly, so motion stays consistent across frames. Conditioning can be text, a starting image (image-to-video), reference images for characters, or audio. Wan2.2's 5B model, for example, generates 720p video at 24 frames per second on a single consumer GPU, while hosted frontier models produce longer, higher-resolution clips with dialogue and sound effects.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Hosted APIs are \u003cb>asynchronous\u003c/b>: you submit a job, poll its status or receive a webhook, then download the result. Generation takes from tens of seconds to minutes, and clips are usually seconds long rather than minutes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The state of play (late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The provider landscape has shifted repeatedly. OpenAI shut down its Sora 2 video API on 24 September 2026 without a one-to-one replacement. Google shut down its Veo 3.1 previews in the Gemini API in October 2026 and points developers to \u003cb>Gemini Omni Flash\u003c/b>, a multimodal model that generates video and edits it conversationally across turns. Open-weight families (Wan and others) keep improving for self-hosting. Lessons for engineers: put video generation behind your own interface, keep prompts and assets portable, and expect to re-evaluate providers every few months.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>World models\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>world model\u003c/b> predicts what happens next in an environment given actions, and can be \"played\". Google DeepMind's \u003cb>Genie 3\u003c/b> (August 2025) generates navigable worlds from a text prompt in real time at 24 frames per second and 720p, staying consistent for a few minutes, and supports \u003cb>promptable world events\u003c/b> such as changing the weather mid-session. Its stated limitations: a constrained set of actions an agent can take directly, and interaction lasting minutes rather than hours. NVIDIA's Cosmos platform applies world models to physical AI such as robots and autonomous vehicles.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Why it matters to AI engineers: world models are becoming \u003cb>simulators for training and testing agents\u003c/b> (robots, game agents, computer-use agents) in situations too rare, costly or dangerous to collect in reality, and an early form of interactive generated media.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\u003cth>Works well today\u003c/th>\u003cth>Watch out for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Marketing and social variants\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short clips, many versions, human review\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Brand consistency, likeness and IP rights\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Previsualisation and storyboards\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Fast iteration on shots\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Physics and continuity errors\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Product and training videos\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Image-to-video from real product shots\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Text in video, exact details\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Synthetic data for vision models\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rare scenarios, varied conditions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Domain gap to real footage\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Costs are per second of output and much higher than images; generation is slow; content policies restrict real people and copyrighted characters; provenance marks (C2PA, watermarks) are standard on major providers. Evaluate with human review of every clip that ships.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What are spacetime patches and why are they used?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Small blocks of a compressed video covering a patch of pixels across a few frames; a diffusion transformer denoises all of them together, which lets it model motion and keep objects consistent across frames, just as image patches let a transformer model an image.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the difference between a video generation model and a world model like Genie 3?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A video model renders a fixed clip from a prompt in one job; a world model generates frames in real time in response to actions, so a user or agent can move through and interact with the generated environment.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Given how often video APIs change, how should you build a product feature on them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Wrap generation behind your own interface with an async job abstraction, keep prompts, reference assets and settings portable, keep an evaluation set of prompts to re-run on new providers, and plan for migration on short notice.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:multimodal/charts-and-screenshots-with-a-vlm":"\u003cp>You will build a tiny benchmark where the right answers are known exactly, because you generate the charts yourself, then measure how resolution and prompting change accuracy and cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+, \u003ccode>pip install openai matplotlib pillow\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>A vision-capable model: a hosted VLM, or a local one through Ollama's OpenAI-compatible endpoint\u003c/li>\u003cli>Five screenshots of real screens you use (a settings page, an invoice, a dashboard), with three field values written down for each\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Generate ten bar charts from random data and save the ground truth:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, random\nimport matplotlib\nmatplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nrandom.seed(7)\nitems = []\nfor i in range(10):\n    labels = random.sample([\"North\", \"South\", \"East\", \"West\", \"Central\", \"Online\"], 5)\n    values = [random.randint(10, 400) for _ in labels]\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 5), dpi=200)          # 1600 x 1000 pixels\n    ax.bar(labels, values, color=\"steelblue\")\n    ax.set_title(\"Sales by region (thousand EUR)\")\n    ax.set_ylabel(\"Sales (thousand EUR)\")\n    fig.savefig(f\"chart_{i}.png\")\n    plt.close(fig)\n    target = random.choice(labels)\n    items.append({\"image\": f\"chart_{i}.png\", \"target\": target,\n                  \"value\": values[labels.index(target)],\n                  \"max_label\": labels[values.index(max(values))]})\njson.dump(items, open(\"items.json\", \"w\"), indent=1)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Ask two questions per chart, requesting JSON so scoring is automatic:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import base64, json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()   # or base_url=\"http://localhost:11434/v1\", api_key=\"ollama\"\n\ndef ask(path: str, question: str) -&gt; tuple[dict, int]:\n    b64 = base64.b64encode(open(path, \"rb\").read()).decode()\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;vision-model&gt;\", temperature=0, messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [\n            {\"type\": \"image_url\", \"image_url\": {\"url\": f\"data:image/png;base64,{b64}\"}},\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": question + ' Reply only with JSON: {\"answer\": ...}'}]}])\n    text = r.choices[0].message.content.strip().removeprefix(\"```json\").removesuffix(\"```\")\n    return json.loads(text), r.usage.prompt_tokens\n\nitems = json.load(open(\"items.json\"))\nfor it in items:\n    v, tok = ask(it[\"image\"], f\"Read the y-axis first. What is the value of the '{it['target']}' bar?\")\n    m, _ = ask(it[\"image\"], \"Which region has the highest value?\")\n    it.update(pred_value=v[\"answer\"], pred_max=m[\"answer\"], tokens=tok)\n    it[\"value_ok\"] = abs(float(it[\"pred_value\"]) - it[\"value\"]) &lt;= 0.05 * it[\"value\"]\n    it[\"max_ok\"] = str(it[\"pred_max\"]).strip().lower() == it[\"max_label\"].lower()\nprint(\"value within 5%:\", sum(i[\"value_ok\"] for i in items), \"/ 10\")\nprint(\"highest bar:\", sum(i[\"max_ok\"] for i in items), \"/ 10\")\nprint(\"mean image prompt tokens:\", sum(i[\"tokens\"] for i in items) / 10)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Make 640 × 400 copies (\u003ccode>Image.open(p).resize((640, 400)).save(...)\u003c/code>) and re-run. Record accuracy and mean prompt tokens for both sizes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Add value labels to the bars (\u003ccode>ax.bar_label(bars)\u003c/code> after \u003ccode>bars = ax.bar(...)\u003c/code>), regenerate, and re-run the value question.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Screenshots: for each of your five screenshots ask for the three recorded fields, at full size and at half size. Score exact matches after trimming whitespace.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Remove \"Read the y-axis first\" from the prompt and re-run the value question once to see whether the read-first instruction matters for your model.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>Write a short table: condition, value accuracy, highest-bar accuracy, screenshot field accuracy, mean prompt tokens.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\"Which bar is highest\" is almost always right; exact values read from bar heights are often off by a few percent, because the model must interpolate between gridlines.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With value labels on the bars, value accuracy should jump: VLMs read printed text far better than they measure geometry. If you control the charts, label them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Downscaling cuts prompt tokens several-fold. Charts with large text survive it; small screenshot text starts to fail, which is your evidence for choosing a resolution per task.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Malformed JSON or extra prose occasionally breaks parsing; in production use structured outputs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why generate the charts yourself instead of collecting real ones?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Ground truth is then exact and free, so scoring can be fully automatic and differences between conditions reflect the model, not labelling errors.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your screenshot accuracy drops from 14/15 to 9/15 at half resolution while tokens fall by 70%. What do you do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep higher resolution for text-heavy screenshots, or crop to the regions containing the fields and send those at full resolution, which recovers accuracy while still saving tokens; use the cheaper size only for tasks with large text.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:voice-agent-for-a-real-task":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a voice agent that completes one real task end to end, for example booking, moving and cancelling appointments for a small clinic or salon, or answering \"where is my order?\" calls against an order database. Callers reach it from a web page (WebRTC) or a phone number. It must feel like a competent receptionist: it answers quickly, can be interrupted, confirms before it changes anything, and leaves a reviewable trace of every call.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Optional Atlas extension:\u003c/b> instead of a new domain, give Atlas a voice front end: callers ask questions answered from Atlas's retrieval with short spoken citations (\"according to the deployment guide\"), and can create a ticket through Atlas's tools with explicit confirmation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Architecture decision\u003c/b> written down: cascaded (Pipecat or LiveKit Agents with streaming STT, LLM and TTS) or speech-to-speech (a realtime API), with the reasons\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tools\u003c/b>: at least three, such as \u003ccode>check_availability\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>create_booking\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cancel_booking\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>lookup_order\u003c/code>, backed by a real store (a database or calendar API), with read-back confirmation and an explicit \"yes\" before any write\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Turn-taking\u003c/b>: semantic or audio turn detection, interruptions with backchannel filtering, history truncated to what the caller heard, and recovery from false interruptions\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Latency\u003c/b>: voice-to-voice p50 under 1 second and p95 under 1.8 seconds over at least 30 turns, measured on the client, with a per-stage breakdown\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Voice-specific prompt\u003c/b>: short replies, no lists or markdown, numbers and dates spoken naturally, a short filler phrase while slow tools run\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tracing\u003c/b>: every call stored with both transcripts, tool calls with arguments and results, per-stage timings and the outcome (Langfuse, Phoenix or structured JSON logs)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Disclosure and privacy\u003c/b>: the agent says it is an AI at the start; credentials for browsers are short-lived; logs keep only the personal data you need\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluation\u003c/b>: 15 scripted scenarios played with synthetic caller audio, plus at least 5 calls by real people\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Echo round trip.\u003c/b> Transport plus STT plus TTS repeating what the caller said. Measure the base latency of your audio path before any LLM is involved.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Brain and tools.\u003c/b> Add the LLM, the voice prompt and the tools against a mock backend. Test single-turn requests by typing, then by voice.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Turn-taking.\u003c/b> Enable the turn detector and interruptions; write the interruption-truncation logic or verify the framework's; test pauses, slow number reading, backchannels and corrections.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Real backend and confirmations.\u003c/b> Connect the real store; add read-back confirmation and idempotent writes so a repeated \"yes\" cannot double-book.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tracing and latency.\u003c/b> Instrument every stage; build a small report of p50 and p95 per stage and end to end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Scenario eval.\u003c/b> Generate caller audio for 15 scenarios with TTS (happy paths, changes of mind, unavailable slots, interruptions, a caller who asks something out of scope, background noise), run them, score task success from the final database state, then run 5 human calls.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix and write up.\u003c/b> Fix the top three failure causes, re-run, and write a one-page report: architecture, latency table, eval results and known limitations.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A caller can complete the task end to end by voice, and the backend state is correct afterwards\u003c/li>\u003cli>The latency report shows p50 under 1 s and p95 under 1.8 s voice-to-voice, with the slowest stage identified\u003c/li>\u003cli>Interrupting the agent stops its speech within about 200 ms, and the trace shows the truncated assistant message\u003c/li>\u003cli>At least 12 of 15 scripted scenarios succeed, with every failure explained in the report\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every call has a reviewable trace with transcripts, tool calls and timings\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Implement the other architecture (cascaded versus speech-to-speech) and compare latency, cost per minute and task success on the same scenarios\u003c/li>\u003cli>Phone access through SIP or a telephony provider, including 8 kHz audio and keypad (DTMF) entry for numbers\u003c/li>\u003cli>Multilingual support with automatic language detection\u003c/li>\u003cli>Escalation to a human with a spoken summary handed over to the human's screen\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Measure before optimising: the echo round trip tells you how much of your budget the network and audio path already use.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the system prompt and tool definitions stable and at the start of the context so prompt caching cuts time to first token.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Names, dates and numbers cause most voice failures: confirm them explicitly, and spell names back letter by letter when they matter.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If p95 latency is dominated by a slow tool, say a short filler line immediately and run the tool in parallel.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
